{
  "where_we_lag": {
    "headline": {
      "text": "Pohjois-Karjala lags nationally in crude shares of curative treatment (21.4%, 95% CI 18.0–25.1 vs 27.0%) and life-prolonging treatment (23.5%, 95% CI 20.1–27.4 vs 30.4%).",
      "claim_ids": [
        "cty:curative:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
        "nat:curative",
        "cty:life_prolonging:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
        "nat:life_prolonging"
      ]
    },
    "details": [
      {
        "text": "In 2019–2023, the county's crude curative treatment share was 21.4% (95% CI 18.0–25.1; n=109/510) vs 27.0% nationally (4009/14873), and its crude life-prolonging treatment share was 23.5% (95% CI 20.1–27.4; n=120/510) vs 30.4% nationally (4522/14873); these 95% confidence intervals exclude the national values.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:curative:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "nat:curative",
          "cty:life_prolonging:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "nat:life_prolonging"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The crude symptomatic treatment share was 52.2% (95% CI 47.8–56.5; n=266/510) vs 39.5% nationally (5871/14873), with a 95% confidence interval excluding the national value; treatment categories overlap because a patient can have several treatments within 12 months, and county shares are not adjusted for age or stage.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:symptomatic:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "nat:symptomatic",
          "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
          "bs:coverage:Treatment_start"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "A multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting was recorded for a crude 0.0% of patients (95% CI 0.0–0.8; n=0/510) vs 6.0% nationally (893/14873), with the 95% confidence interval excluding the national value, though this indicator measures HILMO recording practice from direct patient contacts rather than actual care.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:mdt_recorded:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "nat:mdt_recorded",
          "bs:coverage:MDT",
          "q:39e6ba43a9"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "how_sure_are_we": {
    "headline": {
      "text": "The low crude curative share is unlikely to be only a recording artefact, but county figures are unadjusted for age or stage and omit pathway waiting times.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "cty:curative:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
        "cty:no_treatment_record:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
        "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
        "bs:coverage:Treatment_start"
      ]
    },
    "details": [
      {
        "text": "The crude share with no treatment record within 12 months (mixing untreated patients and unrecorded treatment) is 22.2% (95% CI 18.8–26.0; n=113/510) vs 27.5% nationally (4089/14873), its 95% CI excluding 27.5%; as an inference, this is consistent with relatively complete treatment recording (not direct evidence), so the low crude curative share is less likely to be only a recording artefact.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:no_treatment_record:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "nat:no_treatment_record",
          "cty:curative:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "bs:coverage:Treatment_start"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "A journal article found about 90% HILMO–Cancer Registry diagnosis concordance, which says nothing about treatment or MDT recording; moreover, county shares are crude because stage at diagnosis is missing for ~48% of patients nationally and only digitised national age-adjusted curative reference lines from Kuva 16 (men 26.7%, women 26.9%, about ±0.5 percentage points) are available.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "lit:hilmo_90",
          "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
          "q:7885b0974f",
          "k16:curative:men:national",
          "k16:curative:women:national"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Primary care visits are not included in the report's data and occupational health is not mentioned at all; public data also lack symptom onset, referral dates, imaging dates or waits, and the dates of the first specialist visit and treatment start.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "bs:coverage:Primary_care_occupational_health",
          "q:323de0d90d",
          "bs:coverage:Symptoms",
          "bs:coverage:Referral",
          "bs:coverage:Imaging",
          "bs:coverage:First_specialist_visit",
          "bs:coverage:Treatment_start"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Preliminary, unpublished national figures report an average of 155 days from first symptoms to treatment in Finland (56 days patient delay, 41 days in primary care, and 58 days in specialist care) compared with 107 days in Sweden and 67 days in Denmark, whereas county-level delays remain unmeasured.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "mot:total_fi",
          "mot:total_se",
          "mot:total_dk",
          "mot:patient",
          "mot:primary",
          "mot:specialist"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "what_to_do_first": {
    "headline": {
      "text": "The team recommends reporting THL 21/2016 access times with handover alerts, standardising referrals with a diagnostic contact person, and publishing stage, drug, and MDT recording completeness.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "rec:1",
        "rec:2",
        "rec:3",
        "thl:21_2016",
        "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
        "bs:coverage:MDT"
      ]
    },
    "details": [
      {
        "text": "Because the Health Care Act sets no legal maximum time for a cancer suspicion and national target times are only planned (roadmap pathways in 2026–2028; Filha diagnostic time limits due before the 2030 evaluation), the team recommends reporting referral-to-treatment access times using THL Ohjaus 21/2016 definitions for 16 cancers and flagging stalled handovers.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:1",
          "law:no_max",
          "thl:21_2016",
          "q:def306014c",
          "q:8f358a9de6",
          "q:614febebf2",
          "q:20d6ca414e"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Because mentor insight indicates a weak referral gets a lower urgency rating in specialist care, and while the roadmap plans national referral criteria (2026–2028) and care-coordinator guidance (2026–2028, for county adoption in 2029–2031), the team recommends adopting a KYS-style referral template, referring persistent symptoms after a normal X-ray to a pulmonologist, and naming a diagnostic-phase contact person.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:2",
          "mentor:kaisa_referral",
          "q:9a1cd194f5",
          "q:c61439b30f",
          "q:fc81032a80",
          "q:abcbb06ae3",
          "q:050ee09ebe"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Given Pohjois-Karjala's crude recorded MDT share of 0.0% (95% CI 0.0–0.8; n=0/510) vs 6.0% nationally (893/14873) and missing stage for ~48% of patients nationally—while a national quality registry targeting 90% coverage is planned—the team recommends publishing county recording completeness for stage, drug treatment, and MDT coding and reading gaps in these fields as data-quality signals first.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:3",
          "cty:mdt_recorded:Pohjois_Karjalan_hyvinvointialue",
          "nat:mdt_recorded",
          "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
          "q:7885b0974f",
          "q:950a2f797b",
          "q:f07a34a300",
          "q:4a856bd695"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
